      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "\r\nConsider this:<br>\r\nIf you examine a round yoke sweater it's basically a raglan sweater. Hand knitters get satisfaction (and a unique style) from knitting the yoke in the round. <br>If you can't be bothered with either of the previous methods, consider knitting a raglan sweater and adding the stitch patterning to each piece.<br>The challenge is to work out the <a data-GlossaryId=\"329\" class=\"glossaryhelp\">Stitch Multiples | Stitch Repeats <i class=\"fa fa-question-circle\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a> so the stitch design is seamless.<br>2 options:<br>\r\n<ol>\r\n    <li>Convert a HK top down sweater by \"breaking out\" the raglan shaping from the knitting instructions.\r\n        <ul>\r\n            <li>Chart out the sleeve and body stitch and row counts from the original pattern and knit the piece flat</li>\r\n        </ul>\r\n    </li>\r\n    <li>Machine knit the <a href=\"https://www.knititnow.com/challenge/55/top-down-raglan-collection\">Knit it Now Top Down Raglan Collection</a> and add stitch patterning to the raglan section.</li>\r\n    <li>Use a flat raglan pattern and add a stitch pattern to the yoke section</li>\r\n</ol><br>\r\n<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/101/bottom_up.jpg\">\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 8476,
              "order": 1
            },
